course: dictionary
lesson: 3039
title: Hidden meaning in context: earpiece
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An earpiece is the part you hold to your ear, or put in it, to hear sound. You may not know this word, but the sentences around it give you clues. «Breakfast-show presenters are paid professionals working from a running order and an earpiece; the friendly chat is planned.» The presenters work from a plan and also use something they wear, so an earpiece is probably a small thing that helps them hear.
explain: Read on for a clearer clue. «A voice in their earpiece tells them what comes next.» Someone is talking to the presenter, and the voice comes through the earpiece. So an earpiece is something that brings sound into the ear. Now test your idea: swap in "a voice in their small speaker tells them what comes next". It still makes sense, so you are right.
explain: Words can have more than one meaning. The same word can mean a tiny speaker worn in the ear, or the arm of a pair of glasses that rests over the ear. Both are parts that touch the ear. When you meet a word with two meanings, check which one fits the sentence.
fact: An earpiece is a noun: an earpiece, earpieces.
fact: An earpiece is the part of a phone or headphones that you hold to or put in your ear to hear sound.
fact: A tiny speaker worn in the ear is also called an earpiece. Security guards and TV presenters use them to hear instructions.
fact: «Speak into the earpiece» means talk into the part that you hold near your ear.
fact: The earpiece of a stethoscope goes in the doctor's ears, and one arm of a pair of glasses that rests over the ear is also called an earpiece.
exercise: «Breakfast-show presenters are paid professionals working from a running order and an earpiece.» What is an earpiece? | A type of breakfast cereal | A running shoe | A plate for food on the sofa | *A tiny speaker worn in the ear that brings in instructions
exercise: «A voice in their earpiece tells them what comes next.» Where is the voice coming from? | From the presenter's bag | From the sofa where the guests sit | From the street outside the studio | *From inside the earpiece, in the presenter's ear
exercise: Which part of a pair of glasses is also called an earpiece? | The nose pads | A lens | A frame | *The arm that rests over the ear
test: Security guards wear an earpiece to do what? | Keep their hands warm | Read the newspaper | Cook their lunch | *Hear instructions from their team
test: The word earpiece joins two words. What do they make together? | Earth and piece | Hear and peace | Ear and pie | *Ear and piece, a part that goes by or in the ear
exam: Which everyday use of an earpiece is correct? | *Listening to a phone call or music | Baking a cake in the oven | Measuring the rain | Keeping a drink cold
